   "source": [
    "#3.3\n",
    "\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import scipy.stats as ss\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "\n",
    "def correlation_ratio(categories, measurements):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Calcula o Correlation Ratio (eta) para Numérico vs Categórico\"\"\"\n",
    "    fcat, _ = pd.factorize(categories)\n",
    "    cat_num = np.max(fcat) + 1\n",
    "    y_avg_array = np.zeros(cat_num)\n",
    "    n_array = np.zeros(cat_num)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    for i in range(0, cat_num):\n",
    "        cat_measures = measurements[np.argwhere(fcat == i).flatten()]\n",
    "        n_array[i] = len(cat_measures)\n",
    "        y_avg_array[i] = np.average(cat_measures) if len(cat_measures) > 0 else 0\n",
    "        \n",
    "    y_total_avg = np.average(measurements)\n",
    "    numerator = np.sum(n_array * np.power(y_avg_array - y_total_avg, 2))\n",
    "    denominator = np.sum(np.power(measurements - y_total_avg, 2))\n",
    "    \n",
    "    if denominator == 0:\n",
    "        return 0.0\n",
    "    return np.sqrt(numerator / denominator)\n",
    "\n",
    "def cramers_v(x, y):\n",
    "    confusion_matrix = pd.crosstab(x, y)\n",
    "    chi2 = ss.chi2_contingency(confusion_matrix)[0]\n",
    "    n = confusion_matrix.sum().sum()\n",
    "    phi2 = chi2 / n\n",
    "    r, k = confusion_matrix.shape\n",
    "    \n",
    "    phi2corr = max(0, phi2 - ((k-1)*(r-1))/(n-1))\n",
    "    rcorr = r - ((r-1)**2)/(n-1)\n",
    "    kcorr = k - ((k-1)**2)/(n-1)    \n",
    "    min_dim = min((kcorr-1), (rcorr-1))\n",
    "    if min_dim <= 0:\n",
    "        return 0.0\n",
    "        \n",
    "    return np.sqrt(phi2corr / min_dim)\n",
    "target_col = 'Quadrant'\n",
    "results = {}\n",
    "\n",
    "cols_to_skip = [target_col, 'Audio_Song', 'Lyric_Song', 'AllMusic Id', 'Title', \n",
    "                'SampleURL', 'AppearancesTrackIDs', 'AppearancesAlbumIDs']\n",
    "\n",
    "for col in df_train.columns:\n",
    "    if col in cols_to_skip:\n",
    "        continue\n",
    "        \n",
    "    valid_data = df_train[[col, target_col]].dropna()\n",
    "    \n",
    "    if len(valid_data) == 0 or valid_data[col].nunique() <= 1:\n",
    "        results[col] = 0.0\n",
    "        continue\n",
    "        \n",
    "    if pd.api.types.is_numeric_dtype(valid_data[col]):\n",
    "        score = correlation_ratio(valid_data[target_col].values, valid_data[col].values)\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        score = cramers_v(valid_data[col], valid_data[target_col])\n",
    "        \n",
    "    results[col] = score\n",
    "\n",
    "assoc_df = pd.DataFrame.from_dict(results, orient='index', columns=['Association Score'])\n",
    "assoc_df = assoc_df.sort_values(by='Association Score', ascending=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(12, 8))\n",
    "sns.barplot(x=assoc_df['Association Score'], y=assoc_df.index, hue=assoc_df.index, palette='magma', legend=False)\n",
    "plt.title(\"3.3 — Associação de cada Característica com o Alvo (Quadrant)\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Score de Associação (0 a 1)\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Características\")\n",
    "plt.xlim(0, 1.1)\n",
    "plt.axvline(x=0.8, color='red', linestyle='--', label='Associação Crítica (>0.8)')\n",
    "plt.legend()\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "display(assoc_df.head(10))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5c321e8f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 3.3 & 3.4 - Association Measurement and Target Leakage Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "**3.3 Measure how well each column alone predicts the quadrant.**\n",
    "As shown in the bar chart above, Valence (0.95) and Arousal (0.85) possess an almost perfect association with the target Quadrant. This extreme association is expected, as these two variables mathematically define the quadrants themselves. Interestingly, the next highest associations come from Genres (0.50), Styles (0.47), and Artist (0.46), whereas the expected metadata tags like Themes (0.39) and Moods (0.39) show a moderate association score due to their high categorical cardinality. Regardless of the mathematical scores, the mechanism by which the target reached the Moods and Themes columns is intrinsic to the dataset creation process (mapping AllMusic tags to valence and arousal). Therefore, these metadata fields, along with Valence and Arousal, are not predictive features; they are the source code of the labels themselves.\n",
    "\n",
    "**3.4 Compare 3.3 with the list of 2.3. What proportion was obtainable by reading alone?**\n",
    "Comparing the mathematical results of Section 3.3 with our preliminary assessment in Section 2.3, 100% of the severe target leakage (Valence and Arousal) was obtainable by reading alone. Before running any code, the documentation explicitly stated the labeling methodology. This allowed us to successfully predict in Section 2.3 that Valence, Arousal, Moods, MoodsAll, and Themes would artificially give away the target (as they were used to compute the ground truth). Although Genres, Styles, and Artist showed unexpectedly higher mathematical associations than the mood tags, the core variables responsible for the methodological target leakage were entirely identified simply by reading the documentation. These variables must be discarded from the input space for the machine learning pipeline."
   ]
  },
